+
+## Why these four, in one paragraph each
+
+**R17 — the federation's own skills are already drifting.** `.agents/skills/`
+holds 9 SKILL.md folders whose frontmatter follows at least three conventions:
+bare `name`+`description`; plus `risk: unknown` (concise-planning, fastapi-pro);
+plus `category:` (github-actions-debugger); plus nested `metadata:`
+(developing-genkit-js). Separately, `skills/schema.py` defines a pydantic
+`SkillMetadata` (name/version/description/author) for the dynamic registry that
+shares nothing with the SKILL.md plane. The open standard (progressive disclosure;
+frontmatter minimum `name`+`description`; name==directory) is now the ecosystem's
+convergence point — a stdlib conformance validator turns the drift into
+fail-closed errors before skill loaders mis-discover. Filed as **#1672**
+(labels `P1-high`, `status:unclaimed`, `repo:skills`).
+
+**R18 — stop hand-rolling DevTools.** The `devtools-audit` skill promises console
+errors, network failures, Core Web Vitals and Lighthouse-style auditing, and the
+loop's `human-eye` role must inspect "user-facing public funnel speed
+(FCP < 1.0s)" — but no tool in the control plane produces trace-backed lab data.
+`chrome-devtools-mcp` (54,286★, Apache-2.0, official org) ships exactly this as a
+maintained MCP server + CLI. Adoption is a control-plane adapter plus repointing
+the skill's instructions; the skill's Bangla-report contract stays untouched.
+Filed as **#1673** (`repo:browser`).
+
+**R19 — agent patches deserve tamper-evidence.** The bridge work-order flow
+submits full_file payloads with narrative trust fields. Merged precedent #1644
+shows the accepted extension pattern: an additive fail-closed sub-check in
+`deploy_preflight_guard`. The minimal lane embeds the submitted patch digest in
+the bridge PR body and has the guard recompute it pre-landing (detects
+post-review mutation); the full lane is an admin-owned workflow attesting
+release artifacts via `actions/attest-build-provenance`. Filed as **#1674**
+(`repo:monorepo`).
+
+**R20 — identity is becoming a protocol.** The Web Bot Auth draft is now an IETF
+working-group document; Cloudflare and Akamai already verify the signatures at
+the edge and OpenAI signs Operator with the same scheme. With bot traffic
+measured past human traffic (June 2026), destinations increasingly split between
+"provably identified" and "anonymous" automated traffic. The proposal is an
+explorer probe (Ed25519 keypair, self-signed JWKS directory, Cloudflare test
+endpoint) that produces measured evidence before any runtime commitment — the
+stealth plane stays. Filed as **#1675** (`repo:browser`).
+
+## Considered and held back (zero-hype discipline)
+
+- **llms.txt** — plausible at first look (agent-discovery surface for
+  `supremeai-open`, the "Public Boundary & Ambassador"), but the evidence cut
+  against filing: a Cloud Security Alliance research note (Sep 2026) argues the
+  llms.txt trust model is broken as a supply-chain surface, and independent SEO
+  studies (2026) found no citation benefit. **Verdict: `MONITOR`** — no issue
+  filed. If the federation ever publishes one, it should ship together with an
+  ingestion-side verifier (treat third-party llms.txt as untrusted input).
+- **Post-quantum hybrid TLS on the api plane** — real (ML-KEM hybrids are default
+  at major CDNs) but the gateway does not terminate TLS at a layer agents can
+  change; config-level ops work for the operator, not a federation expansion
+  lane. Held back rather than filed as a hype ticket.
+
